Here are a few ways to write a JavaScript function to validate an email address, ranging from a simple **regex-based check** to a more **robust (but complex) approach**.

### Option 1: Simple & Common (Recommended for most web forms)

This uses a standard, reasonably effective regular expression to check the basic structure of an email address.

```javascript
/**
 * Validates an email address using a common regular expression.
 * @param {string} email The email string to validate.
 * @returns {boolean} True if the email format is valid, false otherwise.
 */
function validateEmailSimple(email) {
  // A common, practical regex for email validation.
  // It checks for: [characters]@[characters].[domain]
  const emailRegex = /^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/;

  if (typeof email !== 'string' || email.trim() === '') {
    return false; // Handle null, undefined, or empty strings
  }

  return emailRegex.test(email.trim());
}

// --- Examples ---
