The most common and robust way is to use `strncpy` (though it has caveats) or to manually iterate and use `memcpy` or direct assignment.

### Method 1: Using `strncpy` (The Standard Library Approach)

`strncpy` is often used as a replacement for `strcpy`, but it has a crucial difference: it **does not guarantee null-termination** if the source string is longer than the destination buffer. You must manually add the null terminator.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

/**
 * Copies a source string to a destination buffer without using strcpy.
 *
 * @param dest The destination buffer (must be large enough).
 * @param src The source string to copy.
 * @param dest_size The size of the destination buffer.
 * @return A pointer to the destination buffer.
 */
char* str_copy_strncpy(char *dest, const char *src, size_t dest_size) {
    if (dest == NULL || src == NULL) {
        return NULL;
    }

    // 1. Copy up to (dest_size - 1) characters
    strncpy(dest, src, dest_size - 1);

    // 2. Manually ensure null termination (CRITICAL step for strncpy)
    dest[dest_size - 1] = '\0';

    return dest;
}

int main() {
    const char *source = "Hello World!";
    char destination[20]; // Buffer size must be large enough

    printf("Original source: %s\n", source);

    str_copy_strncpy(destination, source, sizeof(destination));

    printf("Copied destination: %s\n", destination);

    // Example demonstrating buffer overflow protection (if source is too long)
    const char *long_source = "This string is definitely too long for the buffer.";
    char small_buffer[10];
    str_copy_strncpy(small_buffer, long_source, sizeof(small_buffer));
    printf("\nLong source copied to small buffer: %s\n", small_buffer);

    return 0;
}
```

### Method 2: Manual Iteration (The Fundamental Approach)

This method is the most fundamental. It manually loops through the source string and copies each character one by one into the destination, ensuring the null terminator is placed at the end.

