Here is a complete C function that sorts an integer array using the **Bubble Sort** algorithm, along with a `main` function to demonstrate how to use it.

### C Code for Bubble Sort

```c
#include <stdio.h>

/**
 * @brief Sorts an integer array using the Bubble Sort algorithm.
 *
 * @param arr The integer array to be sorted.
 * @param n The number of elements in the array.
 */
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    int i, j;
    // Outer loop for passes (n-1 passes are sufficient)
    for (i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        // Inner loop for comparisons and swaps in each pass
        // The last 'i' elements are already in place, so we only need to check up to n-i-1
        for (j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            // Compare adjacent elements
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                // Swap arr[j] and arr[j+1]
                int temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                arr[j + 1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}

/**
 * @brief Utility function to print the elements of an array.
 *
 * @param arr The integer array to print.
 * @param size The number of elements in the array.
 */
void printArray(int arr[], int size) {
    int i;
    for (i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);
    }
    printf("\n");
}

// Main function to test the bubbleSort function
int main() {
    // 1. Define the array and its size
    int numbers[] = {64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90};
    int n = sizeof(numbers) / sizeof(numbers[0]);

    printf("Original array: \n");
    printArray(numbers, n);

    // 2. Call the sorting function
    bubbleSort(numbers, n);

    // 3. Print the sorted array
    printf("\nSorted array (using Bubble Sort): \n");
    printArray(numbers, n);

    return 0;
}
```

### Explanation of the Code

#### 1. `void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n)`

* **Parameters:**
    * `arr[]`: The integer array that needs to be sorted.
    * `n`: The total number of elements in the array.
* **Logic (The Bubble Sort Mechanism):**
    * **Outer Loop (`for (i = 0; i < n - 1; i++)`):** This loop controls the number of passes required. After the first pass, the largest element "bubbles up" to the last position. After the second pass, the second-largest element is in its correct place, and so on. We need $n-1$ passes in the worst case.
    * **Inner Loop (`for (j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++)`):** This loop performs the actual comparisons and swaps for the current pass.
        * We iterate from the start (`j=0`) up to the element just before the already sorted

