### 1. Iterative Approach (Most Efficient for Standard Integers)

This is generally the preferred method for calculating Fibonacci numbers because it has a time complexity of $O(n)$ and a space complexity of $O(1)$ (since we only store the last two numbers).

```rust
/// Calculates the n-th Fibonacci number using an iterative approach.
///
/// The sequence starts: F(0) = 0, F(1) = 1, F(2) = 1, F(3) = 2, ...
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `n` - The index of the Fibonacci number to calculate (must be non-negative).
///
/// # Returns
///
/// The n-th Fibonacci number as a u64. Returns 0 if n is negative.
pub fn fibonacci_iterative(n: u32) -> u64 {
    if n == 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    if n == 1 {
        return 1;
    }

    let mut a = 0; // F(i-2)
    let mut b = 1; // F(i-1)
    let mut result = 0;

    // Start loop from i=2 up to n
    for _ in 2..=n {
        result = a + b;
        a = b;
        b = result;
    }

    result
}

